{
 "thread": "9cMEt5BN9dQ",
 "subject": "Is the \"threshold\" a fixed argument or an upper limit in a \"threshold\" series in the mlg.filter() ?",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2018-01-09T00:23:10Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "dcgP1IjODgAJ",
   "from": "Yu NING",
   "author": "fb72064169045944",
   "date": "2017-12-27T13:29:43Z",
   "subject": "Is the \"threshold\" a fixed argument or an upper limit in a \"threshold\" series in the mlg.filter() ?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello, dear fellows<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m now very confused about the &quot;<b>threshold</b>&quot; argument in the<i> mlg.filter() .</i> In the vignett &quot;mlg&quot; ,there is one section about &quot;Thresholds&quot; where I get these two snapshot:</div><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-y92YV5KSOgk/WkOXklXO7WI/AAAAAAAAABg/TgOjAiG1oeELarrD7biR-ElLKwfvPjVtQCLcBGAs/s1600/Threshold2.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-y92YV5KSOgk/WkOXklXO7WI/AAAAAAAAABg/TgOjAiG1oeELarrD7biR-ElLKwfvPjVtQCLcBGAs/s1600/Threshold2.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792480645294000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3rr1KsJxPRyLguR8wuDqn_\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-y92YV5KSOgk/WkOXklXO7WI/AAAAAAAAABg/TgOjAiG1oeELarrD7biR-ElLKwfvPjVtQCLcBGAs/s320/Threshold2.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"320\" height=\"82\"></a></p><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-qn-owZgPyDE/WkOW63rmsCI/AAAAAAAAABU/sHi2L7RmIpQ7cuKRk7Qk7r_OAXhd1D1rACLcBGAs/s1600/Threshold.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-qn-owZgPyDE/WkOW63rmsCI/AAAAAAAAABU/sHi2L7RmIpQ7cuKRk7Qk7r_OAXhd1D1rACLcBGAs/s1600/Threshold.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792480645294000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3fge3902bbET5BUDTMJmkD\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-qn-owZgPyDE/WkOW63rmsCI/AAAAAAAAABU/sHi2L7RmIpQ7cuKRk7Qk7r_OAXhd1D1rACLcBGAs/s320/Threshold.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"320\" height=\"101\"></a></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">In the first picture, &quot;threshold = 0&quot; and the return is &quot;$THRESHOLDS =0&quot;, <b>BUT  </b>in the second picture, &quot;threshold = 1&quot; and the return is &quot;$THRESHOLDS =0&quot; .Why?</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">Then  I look through the example in the <i>poppr</i> manual, I get these codes:</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\">&quot;((thresholds &lt;- mlg.filter(Pinf, distance = bruvo.dist, stats = &quot;THRESHOLDS&quot;,</span>replen = pinfreps, threshold = 1))<br><br>(pcut &lt;- cutoff_predictor(thresholds))&quot;<br></div></div></code></div><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">It seems the &quot;threshold=1&quot;  act to define the upper limit of the thresholds, and the<i> mlg.filter</i> () return a series of  &quot;threshold&quot; value which is conveyed to <i>cutoff_predictor () </i>to find the upper 50% middle value ?</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">So, is it proper to understand &quot;threshold&quot; argument to be a upper limit of a &quot;thresholds&quot; series?  and  the value is only fixed when the assignment expression like </p><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;</span><span style=\"background-color:white;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\">mlg.filter(myData, distance =</span><span style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\">myDist) &lt;- 0.5</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,136,0);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\">&quot;</span></div></code></div><p></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">is used ?<br><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">Any advice is highly appreciated and I really like this package, :-)</p><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "WdA6KA3gAQAJ",
   "from": "Zhian Kamvar",
   "author": "d192ec4b1d416345",
   "date": "2017-12-28T00:58:26Z",
   "subject": "Re: [poppr] Is the \"threshold\" a fixed argument or an upper limit in a \"threshold\" series in the mlg.filter() ?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">Hello,<div><br></div><div>The threshold defines the limit of genetic distance at which individuals would be considered of a different multilocus lineage (according to the algorithm defined).</div><div><br></div><div><div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"clear:both\">In the first picture, &quot;threshold = 0&quot; and the return is &quot;$THRESHOLDS =0&quot;, <b>BUT  </b>in the second picture, &quot;threshold = 1&quot; and the return is &quot;$THRESHOLDS =0&quot; .Why?</p></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div>Technically, THRESHOLDS did not return &quot;0&quot;; it returned &quot;numeric(0)&quot;, which indicates a zero-length numeric vector. In other words, no MLGs were collapsed. </div></div><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"clear:both\">Because mlg.filter() collapses MLGs into lineages, the output of mlg.filter(..., stats = &quot;THRESHOLDS&quot;) gives all of the thresholds at which two MLGs were merged into a lineage. In this example, the smallest genetic distance &gt; 0 is 1. Since this distance represents the lower limit to consider MLLs separate, only the two MLGs that had a 0 genetic distance were collapsed, hence THRESHOLDS == 0. If you had set &quot;threshold = 1.1&quot;, you would have gotten 0 and 1 as the result. </p></div></div><div>Hope that helps,</div><div>Zhian</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><div>\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">-----</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">Zhian N. Kamvar, Ph. D.</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">Postdoctoral Researcher (Everhart Lab)</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">Department of Plant Pathology</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">University of Nebraska-Lincoln</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">ORCID: 0000-0003-1458-7108</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\"><br></div><br></div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<br><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
